From 257121c0b387ceb91ee080869478b41b7613fe86 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: haowen1993 <39072333+haowen1993@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:26:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] feat(i18n): translate card rulings into Chinese in the
 sidecar
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The engine surfaces rulings as `{date, text}` with no id, so they have to be
joined by the English sentence itself. Measured over the WHO pool's 79,668
rulings: a raw comparison finds 89%, and folding the typography the two sources
disagree on (curly vs straight quotes, em dash vs `--`, ellipsis character vs
three dots, incidental whitespace) finds 99%. That fold is `normalizeForMatch` in
the generator and `normalizeRulingText` in the client, which must stay identical.

Two structural decisions, both from getting it wrong first:

  * The sidecar stores rulings as a map keyed by the NORMALIZED ENGLISH TEXT, not
    a positional array. Any single ruling may be untranslated, so an array slides
    out of correspondence and attaches the wrong Chinese sentence to a ruling.
    Keyed, a lookup either matches or falls back to English.
  * The generator now iterates the WHOLE pool rather than only the cards that won
    a Chinese Oracle record. The two datasets cover different sets — text is
    per-Oracle-record, rulings are per-card — and most cards with rulings have no
    Chinese text record at all (13,724 cards carry rulings against 34,522 with
    text). Iterating the text winners alone silently dropped those.

Result: 52,774 ruling translations across 13,724 cards; 34,853 of 35,003 pool
faces now carry an entry. `useCardRulings` overlays per ruling and keeps the
English text for any ruling without a translation, matching the name/oracle-text
overlay's fallback. Stage-0 (`gpt`) translations stay excluded like the card-text
field; `--all-stages` includes them.
